M4.2: AHCI driver - polling DMA storage, LBA round-trips verified

- page.c: page_map_at() - explicit fixed-PA mapping into the demand
  region (so HBA MMIO above the 1 GiB identity map is reachable);
  shared "ensure levels" walk refactored with the demand pager.
  pci.c: pci_cfg32_read exported (bus-master/INTx config from ahci).
- ahci.c: one HBA found by PCI class 010601, polling design (GHC.IE
  off, PCI INTx muted): single outstanding command, one live header
  (CFL=5, PRDTL=1, W bit for writes), h2d FIS built per op, IDENTIFY
  (0xEC) decodes model + LBA48 capacity, ahci_read/ahci_write do 16-
  byte-entry PRDTs for 0x25/0x35. HBA BAR -> demand region +32 MiB;
  command list, command table and rx-FIS each get a pmm frame.
- Encoding facts learned against qemu 7.2 ich9-ahci (all three bit
  me, fixed with the emulation source as ground truth):
  * command header CTBA lives at bytes 8..15 (dwords 2-3); dword 1 is
    the controller-owned PRDBC - putting CTBA in dword 1 made QEMU
    map its FIS from guest address 0 and silently drop every command
    (CI never drained).
  * PRDT entries are SIXTEEN bytes: DW0 DBA, DW1 DBAU, DW2 reserved,
    DW3 DBC|I - PATA-style 8-byte entries parse as 1-byte transfers.
  * sector count lives in FIS bytes 12-13 (byte 11 is feature-hi);
    count shifted one byte over turned "read 8 sectors" into a
    65536-sector (count 0 on LBA48) request of the whole 32 MiB disk.
- main.c: DMA round-trip self-test against build/ahcidisk.img (32 MiB
  raw, created at bench time; marker 'KERNOSDISK\x01\x02\x03' at
  offset 51200 = LBA 100): read LBA 100 and match the marker, then
  write a second pattern to LBA 200 and read it back.

Evidence (QEMU, boot disk on IDE + ich9-ahci + ide-hd on ahci.0):
  ahci: drive 'QEMUHARDDISK' sectors=0x10000  (65536 = exactly the
  32 MiB test disk, real IDENTIFY over DMA)
  ahci: lba100 marker matches
  ahci: lba200 write-verify ok
All previous gates green after: pf/heap/pci/scheduler unchanged.
